menu "HomeControl runtime image"

	config HC_IMAGE_LOAD_YIELD
		bool "Let the lower-priority tasks run between the phases of an image load"
		default y
		help
			An image load runs on the event loop for several hundred milliseconds, at the loop's
			priority. Meanwhile the tasks below it (the KNX socket's reader, mDNS, the E-Home sender,
			the console writer) cannot take what the network delivers to them, and every packet that
			waits for them holds a WiFi receive buffer of the heap: on the C6 about 20 KB in the first
			170 ms of a deploy (homecontrol #70). With this option the loop sleeps one tick at the end
			of each phase (about 20 phases, at most 10 ms each), so they drain what has arrived.

	config HC_IMAGE_HEAP_PHASES
		bool "Log the heap at each phase of an image load"
		default n
		help
			Logs, under hc.image at info level, the free heap and the lowest free heap of each phase
			of every image load: the parse (json, datapoints, schedules, history, ui), the rules'
			and the blocks' compilation, the model, the adoption of the rules and the blocks, the
			bindings, the start and the release of the old image (homecontrol #70, doc/image.md
			"Memory of a swap" lists them). Off, the same
			lines are logged at debug level: `log level hc.image debug` on the console switches them
			on without a rebuild.

	config HC_IMAGE_HEAP_BY_TASK
		bool "With the heap of each task (heap task tracking)"
		default n
		select HEAP_TASK_TRACKING
		help
			Adds to the load's last line and to the lines 3 s and 10 s after it every task whose heap
			changed by 256 B or more since the load began (heap_caps_get_per_task_info): what the
			load's own allocations do not explain, such as network buffers of tasks the load held
			back. Turns on the IDF's heap task tracking, which costs a few bytes per allocation and
			some speed: for measurements, not for production builds.

endmenu
